    "body": "Interesting question :)  In attempt to think about this some, I created [this little page](https://vintage-tachometer.glitch.me/img-map-interop.html) because your question made me think of a few more, and the state of interop/what we can look at...  The page has image, map, image, then a link, and finally a div at the bottom that tells you what has focus via active element.\r\n\r\nIn webkit, chrome and ff for me sequential focus reported at the bottom is always:  area (1), area (2), area(3), link\r\n\r\nI also had some rules that attempt to change stuff about the focus ring, based on the element it thinks has :focus (I'd hope it would match the activeElement, but.. who knows, right?).  In webkit and chrome it is yellow, indicating that the area is matching, and it is drawn on the boxes in the first image - but it seems that's about the only outline property that is respected (color)?  In firefox, it wildly draws focus rings on the area over both sets of images at the same time - which was surprising to me.  It also doesn't seem to respect outline styling there.\r\n\r\nClicking on boxes 1,2,3 on the second image will draw a focus indicator over the respective areas over the first image in webkit and chrome.  It won't draw an indicator at all in firefox.  Devtools pseudo-state forcing focus doesn't seem to draw a ring in any of them. ",
