    "created_at": "2019-10-31T17:28:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-10-31T17:28:02Z",
    "body": "So there are a few issues here:\r\n\r\n1. As described in the spec, a `<map>` can be used for multiple images, so each `<area>` can spawn multiple focusable areas.\r\n\r\n2. The `<area>` element can be defined in a very different part of the DOM than the `<img>` element. It therefore affects focus order whether you're focusing based on the area's own position in the DOM as opposed to focusing as if it was a child of the `<img>`.\r\n\r\n3. For actually handling any events or focus changes, you probably want access to the actual DOM element that defines the link properties (the `<area>`) rather than to the image.\r\n\r\nI am *not* seeing browser interop on any behavior other than the fact that the `activeElement` is always an `<area>`, not an `<img>`, after running a [modified version of Brian's test](https://ebony-alder.glitch.me/):\r\n\r\n- Chrome behaves as Brian described: the areas are added to the focus order based on their position in the DOM, and if an area is focused the focus outline is only drawn around the matching shape in the first image map. The active element and event target are always the area elements, not the image.\r\n\r\n- Firefox gets *really* confused about where to put the areas in the tab order when they are used on two images! (Sometimes it skips over the links in between the two image maps. Sometimes it skips the first image maps, goes to the in-between links, then adds the areas for the second image map. Sometimes the tab order goes in loops.) Regardless, focus outlines are drawn around all the shapes that match the currently focused area when tabbing, focus outlines are never drawn when clicking. The active element and event target are always the area elements, not the image.\r\n\r\n- EdgeHTML and IE seem to match the spec the closest: the shapes are added to the focus order based on the image's position in the DOM, shapes on each element can be focused separately, and the `<img>` is the event target. However, the activeElement is still the `<area>`.\r\n\r\n(I don't have a WebKit browser to test — I assume probably the same as Chrome because I doubt this behavior has been updated in Chromium since the split. But please leave a comment to confirm / correct!)\r\n\r\nI have to say that the IE behavior makes the most sense to me. Not sure if it's possible to sensibly describe that in a modern shadow DOM model. I suppose a more ideal model would to have the focused element actually be a clone of the `<area>` within the image's shadow tree. But then, with event retargetting & closed shadow trees, you'd have no way to access the actual DOM object for the actively focused area unless the image element got additional accessor properties.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
