    "body": "On the note of them being rendered or not:\nIn Chrome, &lt;area&gt;s have `display: inline` by default and, if given CSS borders, sizes, backgrounds and so on, they will show up like any other empty inline element would, at the position that they are in the DOM. (= not on the image itself)\nIn Firefox, &lt;area&gt;s have `display: none !important` by default, but even if you override that to something like `inline`, they don't show up when you style them with CSS.\n\nNote that both browsers apply the CSS `cursor` property to the clickable region on the image when it's set on the &lt;area&gt;.\nPersonally, I think it would be useful to explicitly allow/require this, and maybe some more CSS properties, to apply to the clickable region in case an author<sup>(me!)</sup> wants to highlight them on hover. (Chrome already properly matches `:hover` against the clickable region)\nMaybe the clickable region could be treated as a pseudo-element of some sort that automatically inherits stuff from its originating &lt;area&gt;.",
